==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terh₁-}}
{{ety|en|:bor|fr:triage|tree=1}}
{{bor+|en|fr|triage}}, from {{m|fr|trier||to sort}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹiː.ɑːʒ/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-triage.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹi.ɑʒ/|/tɹiˈɑʒ/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑːʒ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Assessment or sorting according to [[quality]], [[need]], etc., especially to determine how resources will be allocated.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Paul M. Levitt|title=Dark Matters: a Novel|isbn=9780826330345|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=wgW9eXRRl0oC|page=40|passage=Let us think of '''triage''', and remember the word's origins. It began with French wool growers in the eighteenth century, but its most illuminating use comes from eighteenth century coffee bean growers, who sorted their beans into best, middling, and broken. The last category came to be known as ''''triage''' coffee.' In war we attend to the most seriously wounded first, which is how most of [us] understand the word today. {{...}} Therefore to protect the best and the middling, we must sell off our ''''triage''' coffee.'}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2007|author=Jeremy Harding|title=It Migrates to Them|journal=London Review of Books|section=29:5, p. 26
|passage=[Mike Davis] notes that the 'late capitalist '''triage''' of humanity' has 'already taken place'.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} The [[process]] of [[sorting]] [[patient]]s so as to determine the order in which they will be treated (for example, by assigning precedence according to the urgency of [[illness]] or [[injury]]).
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[facility]] within a [[hospital]] that screens [[walk-in]] patients and determines which [[department]] or [[clinic]] to send them to. {{q|See also {{m|en|triage center}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|month=11|author=John Macdonald|title=Question Authority|publisher=Balboa Press|isbn=9781452584645|page=170
|text=I knew where most of the medical supplies were kept. I went to '''triage'''. The room was chaos.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2016-08-24|author=Virginia Frusteri Sollars|title=And Some Will Triumph: Stories of the Mentally Ill in a Correctional Setting and the Nurses Who Care for Them|publisher=Xlibris Corporation|isbn=9781514477649
|text='''Triage''' was busy and noisy, and suddenly I had no energy.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Natalie Grover|title=Looking up health symptoms online less harmful than thought, study says|date=2021-03-29|url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/mar/29/looking-up-health-symptoms-online-less-harmful-than-thought-study-says|passage=Using “Dr Google” for health purposes is controversial. Some have expressed concerns that it can lead to inaccurate diagnoses, bad advice on where to seek treatment ('''triage'''), and increased anxiety (cyberchondria).|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220324055529/https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/mar/29/looking-up-health-symptoms-online-less-harmful-than-thought-study-says}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2023-06-13|author=Gilda Cinnella|author2=Renata Beck|author3=Antonio Malvasi|title=Practical Guide to Simulation in Delivery Room Emergencies|publisher=Springer Nature|isbn=9783031100673|page=129
|text=She was told to hydrate and try some Tylenol and if it got worse, she was recommended to go to '''triage''' for evaluation.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|by extension}} The process of [[prioritize|prioritizing]] [[bug]]s to be [[fix]]ed.
# That which is picked out, especially broken coffee beans.
# {{lb|en|rail|military|British}} A [[marshalling yard]], [[classification yard]].

